    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d101">

                <pb facs="0115" n="103" sameAs="frus:frus1944v05/pg103"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">868.01/554</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum of Telephone Conversation, by the Chief of
                        the Division of Near Eastern Affairs</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Merriam</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1944-04-24">April 24, 1944</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>With reference to the attached memoranda of April 18 and 22, 1944,<anchor n="45"
                        xml:id="d101-f1"/> from NEA<anchor n="46" xml:id="d101-f2"/> to the
                    Secretary, having been informed by Mr. <pb facs="0116" n="104" xml:id="pg104"/>
                        Brown<anchor n="47" xml:id="d101-f3"/> of S<anchor n="48" xml:id="d101-f4"/>
                    that on April 23 the Secretary agreed that we should reply to the Greek request
                    in the sense of the final paragraph of NEA’s memorandum of April 22, at Mr.
                    Alling’s request I called up Mr. Philon<anchor n="49" xml:id="d101-f5"/> of the
                    Greek Embassy and communicated the substance of that paragraph to him.</p>
                <p>Mr. Philon was a little puzzled at first, but finally appeared to understand. He
                    asked to be kept informed of developments and I said that we would pass on to
                    him all the information reaching us regarding this matter that we could make
                    available.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
